# Groups, backreferences, alternation \| and concat \&.

=== \(\) groups and \1 \2 swap them in the replacement
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  :s/\([abc]\)\([efg]\)/\2\1/g	 modifies "af fa bg" to "fa fa gb"
given:  |af fa bg
keys:   :s/\([abc]\)\([efg]\)/\2\1/g<CR>
expect: |fa fa gb

=== \1 in a pattern matches the same text again
ref:    pattern.txt */\1*
quote:  Example: "\([a-z]\).\1" matches "ata", "ehe", "tot", etc.
given:  |abc ata
keys:   :s/\([a-z]\).\1/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |abc (ata)

=== \2 in a pattern uses the second group
ref:    pattern.txt */\2*
quote:  Like "\1", but uses second sub-expression,
given:  |ab-ba ab-ab
keys:   :s/\(a\)\(b\)-\2\1/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(ab-ba) ab-ab

=== \9 refers to the ninth group
ref:    pattern.txt */\9*
quote:  Like "\1", but uses ninth sub-expression.
given:  |abcdefghi
keys:   :s/\(a\)\(b\)\(c\)\(d\)\(e\)\(f\)\(g\)\(h\)\(i\)/\9\8\1/<CR>
expect: |iha

=== groups are numbered by where \( comes, not what matched first
ref:    pattern.txt */\9*
quote:  The numbering of groups is done based on which "\(" comes first in the pattern (going left to right), NOT based on what is matched first.
given:  |ab
keys:   :s/\(\(a\)b\)/\2-\1/<CR>
expect: |a-ab

=== a repeated group keeps its last match
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  :s/\(\(a[a-d] \)*\)/\2/      modifies "aa ab x" to "ab x"
given:  |aa ab x
keys:   :s/\(\(a[a-d] \)*\)/\2/<CR>
expect: |ab x

=== a group in an alternative that did not match is empty
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  :s/\([ab]\)\|\([cd]\)/\1x/g   modifies "a b c d"  to "ax bx x x"
given:  |a b c d
keys:   :s/\([ab]\)\|\([cd]\)/\1x/g<CR>
expect: |ax bx x x

=== \%(\) groups without counting
ref:    pattern.txt */\%(\)*
quote:  Just like \(\), but without counting it as a sub-expression.
given:  |ab
keys:   :s/\%(a\)\(b\)/\1/<CR>
expect: |b

=== ^ inside \( is start-of-line
ref:    pattern.txt */\(*
quote:  E.g., "\(^a\)" matches 'a' at the start of a line.
given:  |aba
keys:   :s/\(^a\)/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(a)ba

=== \| matches either branch
ref:    pattern.txt */bar*
quote:  Example: "foo\|beep" matches "foo" and matches "beep".
given:  |foo x beep
keys:   :s/foo\|beep/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(foo) x (beep)

=== \| uses the first branch that matches, not the longest
ref:    pattern.txt */bar*
quote:  If more than one branch matches, the first one is used.
given:  |foobar
keys:   :s/foo\|foobar/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(foo)bar

=== \| inside a group
ref:    pattern.txt */bar*
quote:  A pattern is one or more branches, separated by "\|".
given:  |cat cut cot
keys:   :s/c\(a\|o\)t/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(cat) cut (cot)

=== \& matches the last concat where all match
ref:    pattern.txt */\&*
quote:  "\"foobeep\\&...\" matches \"foo\" in \"foobeep\"."
given:  |foobeep
keys:   :s/foobeep\&.../(&)/<CR>
expect: |(foo)beep

=== \& requires every concat to match
ref:    pattern.txt */\&*
quote:  "\".*Peter\\&.*Bob\" matches in a line containing both \"Peter\" and \"Bob\""
given:
  |Bob only
  Bob and Peter
keys:   :%s/.*Peter\&.*Bob/X/<CR>
expect:
  Bob only
  |X and Peter

=== \& is the same as \@=
ref:    pattern.txt */zero-width*
quote:  Note that using "\&" works the same as using "\@=": "foo\&.." is the same as "\(foo\)\@=..".
given:  |fox foo
keys:   :s/foo\&../(&)/<CR>
expect: |fox (fo)o
